('FI.LM1.01','fi','Spot, forward and par curves','Three upward-sloping curves: the forward curve highest and steepest, the spot curve between, and the par curve lowest and flattest.','0 0 520 175','<line class="grid" x1="34" y1="26" x2="34" y2="122"/><line class="grid" x1="34" y1="122" x2="230" y2="122"/><text class="lab" x="132" y="137" text-anchor="middle">maturity (years)</text><text class="lab" x="18" y="74" transform="rotate(-90 18 74)" text-anchor="middle">rate %</text><path d="M37.9 95.7 L40.7 93.9 L43.4 92.1 L46.2 90.4 L48.9 88.8 L51.6 87.3 L54.4 85.9 L57.1 84.5 L59.9 83.2 L62.6 81.9 L65.4 80.7 L68.1 79.5 L70.8 78.4 L73.6 77.4 L76.3 76.4 L79.1 75.5 L81.8 74.6 L84.6 73.7 L87.3 72.9 L90.1 72.1 L92.8 71.3 L95.5 70.6 L98.3 69.9 L101.0 69.3 L103.8 68.7 L106.5 68.1 L109.3 67.5 L112.0 67.0 L114.8 66.5 L117.5 66.0 L120.2 65.5 L123.0 65.1 L125.7 64.7 L128.5 64.3 L131.2 63.9 L134.0 63.5 L136.7 63.2 L139.4 62.8 L142.2 62.5 L144.9 62.2 L147.7 61.9 L150.4 61.6 L153.2 61.4 L155.9 61.1 L158.7 60.9 L161.4 60.7 L164.1 60.4 L166.9 60.2 L169.6 60.0 L172.4 59.9 L175.1 59.7 L177.9 59.5 L180.6 59.3 L183.4 59.2 L186.1 59.0 L188.8 58.9 L191.6 58.8 L194.3 58.6 L197.1 58.5 L199.8 58.4 L202.6 58.3 L205.3 58.2 L208.0 58.1 L210.8 58.0 L213.5 57.9 L216.3 57.8 L219.0 57.7 L221.8 57.6 L224.5 57.6 L227.3 57.5 L230.0 57.4" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.9" fill="none"/><path d="M37.9 93.3 L40.7 90.0 L43.4 86.9 L46.2 84.0 L48.9 81.3 L51.6 78.8 L54.4 76.5 L57.1 74.3 L59.9 72.3 L62.6 70.4 L65.4 68.7 L68.1 67.1 L70.8 65.6 L73.6 64.2 L76.3 62.9 L79.1 61.7 L81.8 60.6 L84.6 59.5 L87.3 58.6 L90.1 57.7 L92.8 56.8 L95.5 56.0 L98.3 55.3 L101.0 54.7 L103.8 54.0 L106.5 53.4 L109.3 52.9 L112.0 52.4 L114.8 51.9 L117.5 51.5 L120.2 51.1 L123.0 50.7 L125.7 50.4 L128.5 50.0 L131.2 49.7 L134.0 49.5 L136.7 49.2 L139.4 49.0 L142.2 48.7 L144.9 48.5 L147.7 48.3 L150.4 48.1 L153.2 48.0 L155.9 47.8 L158.7 47.7 L161.4 47.5 L164.1 47.4 L166.9 47.3 L169.6 47.2 L172.4 47.1 L175.1 47.0 L177.9 46.9 L180.6 46.8 L183.4 46.7 L186.1 46.7 L188.8 46.6 L191.6 46.5 L194.3 46.5 L197.1 46.4 L199.8 46.4 L202.6 46.3 L205.3 46.3 L208.0 46.3 L210.8 46.2 L213.5 46.2 L216.3 46.2 L219.0 46.1 L221.8 46.1 L224.5 46.1 L227.3 46.0 L230.0 46.0" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none" stroke-dasharray="5 3"/><path d="M37.9 95.8 L40.7 94.0 L43.4 92.2 L46.2 90.6 L48.9 89.1 L51.6 87.6 L54.4 86.2 L57.1 84.8 L59.9 83.6 L62.6 82.3 L65.4 81.2 L68.1 80.1 L70.8 79.1 L73.6 78.1 L76.3 77.1 L79.1 76.2 L81.8 75.4 L84.6 74.6 L87.3 73.8 L90.1 73.1 L92.8 72.4 L95.5 71.7 L98.3 71.1 L101.0 70.5 L103.8 69.9 L106.5 69.4 L109.3 68.9 L112.0 68.4 L114.8 67.9 L117.5 67.5 L120.2 67.1 L123.0 66.7 L125.7 66.3 L128.5 65.9 L131.2 65.6 L134.0 65.2 L136.7 64.9 L139.4 64.6 L142.2 64.4 L144.9 64.1 L147.7 63.8 L150.4 63.6 L153.2 63.4 L155.9 63.2 L158.7 62.9 L161.4 62.7 L164.1 62.6 L166.9 62.4 L169.6 62.2 L172.4 62.1 L175.1 61.9 L177.9 61.8 L180.6 61.6 L183.4 61.5 L186.1 61.4 L188.8 61.2 L191.6 61.1 L194.3 61.0 L197.1 60.9 L199.8 60.8 L202.6 60.7 L205.3 60.6 L208.0 60.6 L210.8 60.5 L213.5 60.4 L216.3 60.3 L219.0 60.3 L221.8 60.2 L224.5 60.1 L227.3 60.1 L230.0 60.0" stroke="var(--violet)" stroke-width="1.4" fill="none" stroke-dasharray="2 2"/><text class="lab" x="242" y="46" fill="var(--amber)">forward &#8212; steepest</text><text class="lab" x="242" y="64" fill="var(--cyan)">spot</text><text class="lab" x="242" y="82" fill="var(--violet)">par &#8212; flattest</text><text class="lab" x="242" y="106">upward-sloping curve:</text><text class="lab ok" x="242" y="120">forward &#62; spot &#62; par</text><text class="lab wn" x="242" y="138">inverted curve: order reverses</text><text class="lab" x="34" y="160">the forward curve always lies above the spot curve when spot is rising, and below when falling</text>','The ordering is the exam''s favourite question and it follows from arithmetic, not memory. A spot rate is an average of the forwards up to that maturity, so if spot is rising the marginal forward must sit above the average. Reverse the curve slope and the whole ordering reverses with it.',ARRAY['FI.LM1.a','FI.LM1.b']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM1.02','fi','The forward rate model','A timeline showing three consecutive one-year forward rates spanning years zero to three, compared with a single three-year spot rate covering the same period.','0 0 540 165','<text class="ttl" x="24" y="20">A long-dated spot rate is built from short ones</text><line class="grid" x1="30" y1="60" x2="330" y2="60"/><line class="grid" x1="30" y1="55" x2="30" y2="65"/><text class="lab" x="30" y="78" text-anchor="middle">0</text><line class="grid" x1="130" y1="55" x2="130" y2="65"/><text class="lab" x="130" y="78" text-anchor="middle">1</text><line class="grid" x1="230" y1="55" x2="230" y2="65"/><text class="lab" x="230" y="78" text-anchor="middle">2</text><line class="grid" x1="330" y1="55" x2="330" y2="65"/><text class="lab" x="330" y="78" text-anchor="middle">3</text><path class="edge edge-y" d="M36 46 H124" marker-end="url(#g)"/><text class="el" x="80" y="40" text-anchor="middle">S&#8321;</text><path class="edge edge-y" d="M136 46 H224" marker-end="url(#g)"/><text class="el" x="180" y="40" text-anchor="middle">f(1,1)</text><path class="edge edge-y" d="M236 46 H324" marker-end="url(#g)"/><text class="el" x="280" y="40" text-anchor="middle">f(2,1)</text><path class="edge" d="M36 96 H324" marker-end="url(#c)"/><text class="el" x="180" y="112" text-anchor="middle">S&#8323; &#8212; the three-year spot</text><text class="nt" x="356" y="52">(1+S&#8323;)&#179; =</text><text class="nt" x="356" y="70">(1+S&#8321;)(1+f(1,1))(1+f(2,1))</text><text class="lab" x="356" y="94">compounding one-period rates</text><text class="lab" x="356" y="108">must equal compounding the</text><text class="lab" x="356" y="122">long rate &#8212; or arbitrage exists</text><text class="lab wn" x="24" y="148">f(2,1) reads: a one-year rate, agreed today, starting two years from now</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','Two routes from today to year three must cost the same: rolling three one-year rates, or locking one three-year rate. That equality is the forward rate model, and every forward rate question is an application of it. Read f(2,1) as the rate for a one-year loan beginning in two years.',ARRAY['FI.LM1.a']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM1.03','fi','Riding the yield curve','An upward-sloping yield curve with a bond purchased at five years rolling down to the four-year point at a lower yield.','0 0 520 175','<line class="grid" x1="34" y1="26" x2="34" y2="122"/><line class="grid" x1="34" y1="122" x2="230" y2="122"/><text class="lab" x="132" y="137" text-anchor="middle">maturity</text><text class="lab" x="18" y="74" transform="rotate(-90 18 74)" text-anchor="middle">yield</text><path d="M37.9 98.9 L40.7 97.2 L43.4 95.6 L46.2 94.0 L48.9 92.5 L51.6 91.1 L54.4 89.7 L57.1 88.3 L59.9 87.1 L62.6 85.9 L65.4 84.7 L68.1 83.6 L70.8 82.5 L73.6 81.5 L76.3 80.5 L79.1 79.5 L81.8 78.6 L84.6 77.8 L87.3 76.9 L90.1 76.1 L92.8 75.4 L95.5 74.6 L98.3 73.9 L101.0 73.2 L103.8 72.6 L106.5 72.0 L109.3 71.4 L112.0 70.8 L114.8 70.3 L117.5 69.7 L120.2 69.2 L123.0 68.7 L125.7 68.3 L128.5 67.8 L131.2 67.4 L134.0 67.0 L136.7 66.6 L139.4 66.2 L142.2 65.9 L144.9 65.5 L147.7 65.2 L150.4 64.9 L153.2 64.6 L155.9 64.3 L158.7 64.0 L161.4 63.7 L164.1 63.5 L166.9 63.2 L169.6 63.0 L172.4 62.8 L175.1 62.5 L177.9 62.3 L180.6 62.1 L183.4 61.9 L186.1 61.8 L188.8 61.6 L191.6 61.4 L194.3 61.2 L197.1 61.1 L199.8 60.9 L202.6 60.8 L205.3 60.7 L208.0 60.5 L210.8 60.4 L213.5 60.3 L216.3 60.2 L219.0 60.1 L221.8 60.0 L224.5 59.8 L227.3 59.8 L230.0 59.7" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.8" fill="none"/><circle cx="132.0" cy="67.3" r="4" fill="var(--amber)"/><circle cx="112.4" cy="70.7" r="4" fill="var(--green)"/><path class="edge edge-y" d="M132.0 59.3 Q122.2 45.3 115.4 62.7" marker-end="url(#g)"/><text class="lab wn" x="138.0" y="81.3">buy 5y</text><text class="lab ok" x="106.4" y="56.7" text-anchor="end">sell as 4y</text><text class="ttl" x="252" y="40">The bet</text><text class="lab" x="252" y="58">the curve stays as it is</text><text class="lab" x="252" y="74">as the bond ages it rolls down</text><text class="lab" x="252" y="90">to a lower yield &#8594; price rises</text><text class="lab ok" x="252" y="112">return = coupon + rolldown</text><text class="lab bad" x="252" y="132">fails if the curve rises or flattens</text><text class="lab" x="34" y="160">works best on a steep, upward-sloping curve that is expected to stay put</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','Buy long, sell before maturity, and pocket the price gain as the bond rolls to a shorter, lower-yielding point. The strategy is an explicit bet that the curve does not move &mdash; a steepening or parallel rise destroys it, which is why it works best when the curve is steep and stable.',ARRAY['FI.LM1.c']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM1.04','fi','Spreads and what they measure','Horizontal bars of differing lengths measured from a Treasury baseline, showing swap spread, TED spread, Libor-OIS spread and Z-spread.','0 0 620 200','<line class="grid" x1="150" y1="24" x2="150" y2="152"/><rect class="node" x="150" y="30" width="0" height="18" rx="2"/><text class="lab" x="144" y="43" text-anchor="end">Treasury</text><text class="nm" x="158" y="43">risk-free benchmark</text><rect class="bar-t" x="150" y="60" width="56" height="18" rx="2"/><text class="lab" x="144" y="73" text-anchor="end">Swap spread</text><text class="nm" x="214" y="73">swap rate &#8722; Treasury &#183; banking-sector credit</text><rect class="bar-t" x="150" y="90" width="84" height="18" rx="2"/><text class="lab" x="144" y="103" text-anchor="end">TED spread</text><text class="nm" x="242" y="103">LIBOR &#8722; T-bill &#183; interbank credit risk</text><rect class="bar-r" x="150" y="120" width="44" height="18" rx="2"/><text class="lab" x="144" y="133" text-anchor="end">Libor&#8211;OIS</text><text class="nm" x="202" y="133">LIBOR &#8722; OIS &#183; money-market liquidity</text><rect class="bar-e" x="150" y="150" width="132" height="18" rx="2"/><text class="lab" x="144" y="163" text-anchor="end">Z-spread</text><text class="nm" x="290" y="163">constant spread over every spot rate</text><text class="ttl" x="24" y="176">Higher spread = more perceived credit or liquidity risk</text><text class="lab wn" x="24" y="192">TED and Libor&#8211;OIS widen sharply in a funding crisis; the swap spread reflects bank credit, not sovereign</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','All four measure a gap over a risk-free base, and the exam tests which risk each one captures. TED is interbank credit; Libor&ndash;OIS is money-market liquidity; the swap spread is banking-sector credit; the Z-spread is the constant addition to every spot rate that makes discounted cash flows equal the market price.',ARRAY['FI.LM1.d','FI.LM1.e']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM1.05','fi','Traditional term structure theories','Four panels, each naming a term structure theory with a small yield curve sketch and its central claim.','0 0 660 205','<g transform="translate(16,22)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="150" height="30" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="75" y="19" text-anchor="middle">Pure expectations</text><line class="grid" x1="10" y1="42" x2="10" y2="106"/><line class="grid" x1="10" y1="106" x2="140" y2="106"/><path d="M14 96 L48 78 L82 84 L116 68" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/><text class="lab" x="0" y="128">forwards = expected spot rates</text><text class="lab wn" x="0" y="142">any shape possible</text></g><g transform="translate(178,22)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="150" height="30" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="75" y="19" text-anchor="middle">Local expectations</text><line class="grid" x1="10" y1="42" x2="10" y2="106"/><line class="grid" x1="10" y1="106" x2="140" y2="106"/><path d="M14 96 L48 82 L82 86 L116 74" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/><text class="lab" x="0" y="128">risk-neutral only short-term</text><text class="lab wn" x="0" y="142">near-term returns equal</text></g><g transform="translate(340,22)"><rect class="node-c" x="0" y="0" width="150" height="30" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="75" y="19" text-anchor="middle">Liquidity preference</text><line class="grid" x1="10" y1="42" x2="10" y2="106"/><line class="grid" x1="10" y1="106" x2="140" y2="106"/><path d="M14 100 L48 80 L82 68 L116 58" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/><text class="lab" x="0" y="128">expectations + term premium</text><text class="lab wn" x="0" y="142">upward bias</text></g><g transform="translate(502,22)"><rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="150" height="30" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="75" y="19" text-anchor="middle">Segmented markets</text><line class="grid" x1="10" y1="42" x2="10" y2="106"/><line class="grid" x1="10" y1="106" x2="140" y2="106"/><path d="M14 92 L48 68 L82 90 L116 62" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/><text class="lab" x="0" y="128">supply and demand by maturity</text><text class="lab wn" x="0" y="142">no smooth shape required</text></g><text class="lab" x="16" y="196">preferred habitat sits between segmented markets and liquidity preference: investors leave their maturity only if paid to</text>','The distinguishing question is always what the forward rate contains. Pure expectations says nothing but expected future spot rates. Liquidity preference adds a term premium that grows with maturity, which biases the curve upward. Segmented markets denies a single mechanism entirely.',ARRAY['FI.LM1.f']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM1.06','fi','Yield curve factor models','Three panels each showing a base yield curve and a shifted version: a parallel move, a steepening, and a change in curvature.','0 0 660 190','<g transform="translate(20,22)"><text class="ttl" x="0" y="0">Level &#8212; parallel shift</text><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="12" x2="0" y2="98"/><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="98" x2="168" y2="98"/><text class="lab" x="84" y="113" text-anchor="middle">maturity</text><path d="M6 78 L48 62 L96 52 L162 46" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-width="1.4" fill="none"/><path d="M6 60 L48 44 L96 34 L162 28" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.7" fill="none" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text class="lab" x="0" y="122">~ most of the variance</text><text class="lab" x="0" y="136">all maturities move together</text></g><g transform="translate(232,22)"><text class="ttl" x="0" y="0">Steepness</text><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="12" x2="0" y2="98"/><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="98" x2="168" y2="98"/><text class="lab" x="84" y="113" text-anchor="middle">maturity</text><path d="M6 78 L48 62 L96 52 L162 46" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-width="1.4" fill="none"/><path d="M6 84 L48 62 L96 44 L162 30" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.7" fill="none" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text class="lab" x="0" y="122">second factor</text><text class="lab" x="0" y="136">short and long move apart</text></g><g transform="translate(444,22)"><text class="ttl" x="0" y="0">Curvature</text><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="12" x2="0" y2="98"/><line class="grid" x1="0" y1="98" x2="168" y2="98"/><text class="lab" x="84" y="113" text-anchor="middle">maturity</text><path d="M6 78 L48 62 L96 52 L162 46" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-width="1.4" fill="none"/><path d="M6 72 L48 74 L96 46 L162 40" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.7" fill="none" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text class="lab" x="0" y="122">third factor</text><text class="lab" x="0" y="136">the middle moves against the wings</text></g><text class="lab wn" x="20" y="178">a butterfly trade isolates curvature; a bullet or barbell positions for steepness</text>','Nearly all yield curve movement decomposes into these three. Level dominates the variance, which is why effective duration &mdash; a parallel-shift measure &mdash; captures most risk. Steepness and curvature are what key rate durations exist to measure, because duration alone is blind to them.',ARRAY['FI.LM1.g','FI.LM1.h']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM2.01','fi','The binomial interest rate tree','A recombining binomial lattice of interest rates branching up and down over three periods, with adjacent nodes related by two standard deviations of volatility.','0 0 600 230','<path class="edge" d="M78 118 L166 74 M78 118 L166 162"/><path class="edge" d="M186 74 L274 46 M186 74 L274 118 M186 162 L274 118 M186 162 L274 190"/><path class="edge" d="M294 46 L382 30 M294 46 L382 74 M294 118 L382 74 M294 118 L382 162 M294 190 L382 162 M294 190 L382 206"/><circle cx="62" cy="118" r="17" class="node-c"/><text class="nm" x="62" y="121" text-anchor="middle">i&#8320;</text><circle cx="176" cy="74" r="19" class="node-c"/><text class="nm" x="176" y="77" text-anchor="middle">i&#8321;,&#8341;</text><circle cx="176" cy="162" r="19" class="node-c"/><text class="nm" x="176" y="165" text-anchor="middle">i&#8321;,&#8343;</text><circle cx="284" cy="46" r="19" class="node-c"/><text class="nm" x="284" y="49" text-anchor="middle">i&#8322;,&#8341;&#8341;</text><circle cx="284" cy="118" r="19" class="node-c"/><text class="nm" x="284" y="121" text-anchor="middle">i&#8322;,&#8341;&#8343;</text><circle cx="284" cy="190" r="19" class="node-c"/><text class="nm" x="284" y="193" text-anchor="middle">i&#8322;,&#8343;&#8343;</text><circle cx="392" cy="30" r="17" class="node"/><circle cx="392" cy="74" r="17" class="node"/><circle cx="392" cy="162" r="17" class="node"/><circle cx="392" cy="206" r="17" class="node"/><text class="el" x="118" y="88" text-anchor="middle">up</text><text class="el" x="118" y="152" text-anchor="middle">down</text><text class="lab" x="62" y="152" text-anchor="middle">t=0</text><text class="lab" x="176" y="212" text-anchor="middle">t=1</text><text class="lab" x="284" y="222" text-anchor="middle">t=2</text><line class="grid" x1="440" y1="20" x2="440" y2="212"/><text class="ttl" x="460" y="34">Two rules</text><text class="nt" x="460" y="58">i&#8341; = i&#8343; &#215; e&#178;&#963;</text><text class="lab" x="460" y="74">adjacent rates differ by</text><text class="lab" x="460" y="88">two standard deviations</text><text class="nt" x="460" y="116">p = 50% up, 50% down</text><text class="lab" x="460" y="132">risk-neutral probabilities,</text><text class="lab" x="460" y="146">not real-world ones</text><text class="lab wn" x="460" y="174">the tree is recombining:</text><text class="lab wn" x="460" y="188">up-then-down lands on the</text><text class="lab wn" x="460" y="202">same node as down-then-up</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','The tree is the engine behind every embedded-option valuation. Two properties do the work: adjacent rates at a date differ by e raised to two sigma, and the tree recombines so up-down and down-up arrive at the same node. Recombination is why an n-period tree has n+1 terminal nodes rather than 2 to the n.',ARRAY['FI.LM2.c','FI.LM2.d']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM2.02','fi','Calibration and backward induction','Top: a calibration loop that guesses node rates, values a benchmark bond and adjusts until it matches market price. Bottom: five backward induction steps from maturity to time zero.','0 0 640 215','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="18">Calibrate forward &#8212; then value backward</text><rect class="node" x="20" y="30" width="132" height="38" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="86" y="47" text-anchor="middle">1. Guess the rates</text><text class="nm" x="86" y="59" text-anchor="middle">at each node</text><path class="edge" d="M152 49 H192" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect class="node" x="192" y="30" width="144" height="38" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="264" y="47" text-anchor="middle">2. Value a benchmark</text><text class="nm" x="264" y="59" text-anchor="middle">option-free bond</text><path class="edge" d="M336 49 H376" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect class="node" x="376" y="30" width="140" height="38" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="446" y="47" text-anchor="middle">3. Compare to market</text><text class="nm" x="446" y="59" text-anchor="middle">does it match par?</text><path class="edge edge-n" d="M446 68 V88 H86 V68" marker-end="url(#m)"/><text class="el" x="266" y="84" text-anchor="middle">no &#8212; adjust and repeat</text><path class="edge edge-y" d="M516 49 H556" marker-end="url(#g)"/><rect class="node-ok" x="556" y="32" width="66" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="589" y="53" text-anchor="middle">calibrated</text><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="106" x2="620" y2="106"/><text class="ttl" x="20" y="128">Backward induction</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="146">start at maturity, where every node pays par plus coupon</text><rect class="node" x="20" y="156" width="106" height="42" rx="2"/><text class="nm" x="73" y="174" text-anchor="middle">t = maturity</text><text class="nm" x="73" y="188" text-anchor="middle">V = 100 + coupon</text><rect class="node-c" x="138" y="156" width="106" height="42" rx="2"/><text class="nm" x="191" y="174" text-anchor="middle">discount back</text><text class="nm" x="191" y="188" text-anchor="middle">at that node&#8217;s rate</text><rect class="node-c" x="256" y="156" width="106" height="42" rx="2"/><text class="nm" x="309" y="174" text-anchor="middle">average the two</text><text class="nm" x="309" y="188" text-anchor="middle">0.5 up + 0.5 down</text><rect class="node-c" x="374" y="156" width="106" height="42" rx="2"/><text class="nm" x="427" y="174" text-anchor="middle">add the coupon</text><text class="nm" x="427" y="188" text-anchor="middle">repeat one step left</text><rect class="node-ok" x="492" y="156" width="106" height="42" rx="2"/><text class="nm" x="545" y="174" text-anchor="middle">reach t = 0</text><text class="nm" x="545" y="188" text-anchor="middle">that is the value</text><path class="edge" d="M126 177 H136" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M244 177 H254" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M362 177 H372" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M480 177 H490" marker-end="url(#a)"/><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','Calibration runs forward and iterates; valuation runs backward and does not. The tree must first reprice option-free benchmarks exactly &mdash; that is what makes it arbitrage-free &mdash; before it can be trusted to value anything with an option attached.',ARRAY['FI.LM2.d','FI.LM2.e']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM2.03','fi','Term structure models','Two boxes contrasting equilibrium models such as CIR and Vasicek with arbitrage-free models such as Ho-Lee, followed by the stochastic differential equations for CIR and Vasicek.','0 0 640 220','<rect class="node-c" x="20" y="24" width="290" height="92" rx="2"/><text class="ttl" x="34" y="44">Equilibrium models</text><text class="lab" x="34" y="62">start from economic assumptions, derive the curve</text><text class="nt" x="34" y="82">CIR &#183; Vasicek</text><text class="lab bad" x="34" y="102">will not reprice today&#8217;s bonds exactly</text><rect class="node-ok" x="330" y="24" width="290" height="92" rx="2"/><text class="ttl" x="344" y="44">Arbitrage-free models</text><text class="lab" x="344" y="62">start from today&#8217;s observed prices, fit the curve</text><text class="nt" x="344" y="82">Ho&#8211;Lee &#183; Kalotay&#8211;Williams&#8211;Fabozzi</text><text class="lab ok" x="344" y="102">reprices today&#8217;s bonds by construction</text><text class="ttl" x="20" y="142">The distinguishing features</text><text class="nt" x="20" y="164">CIR: dr = a(b &#8722; r)dt + &#963;&#8730;r &#183; dz</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="180">mean-reverting, and volatility rises with the level of rates</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="194">&#8212; so rates cannot go negative</text><text class="nt" x="330" y="164">Vasicek: dr = a(b &#8722; r)dt + &#963;dz</text><text class="lab" x="330" y="180">mean-reverting, but volatility is constant</text><text class="lab bad" x="330" y="194">&#8212; so rates can go negative</text>','The exam separates these on two axes. Equilibrium versus arbitrage-free is about whether the model must reprice today''s bonds. CIR versus Vasicek is about the volatility term: the square root of r in CIR makes volatility rise with rates and keeps them positive, whereas Vasicek''s constant sigma permits negative rates.',ARRAY['FI.LM2.h']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM2.04','fi','Pathwise valuation and Monte Carlo','Left: four enumerated binomial paths. Right: six simulated random rate paths.','0 0 620 200','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="18">Pathwise &#8212; enumerate every route</text><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="26" x2="20" y2="122"/><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="122" x2="250" y2="122"/><path d="M26 106 L82 84 L138 62 L194 46" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.2" fill="none" opacity=".75"/><path d="M26 106 L82 84 L138 96 L194 78" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.2" fill="none" opacity=".75"/><path d="M26 106 L82 116 L138 96 L194 74" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.2" fill="none" opacity=".75"/><path d="M26 106 L82 116 L138 124 L194 112" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.2" fill="none" opacity=".75"/><text class="lab" x="20" y="140">2&#8319; paths for n periods &#8212; average their present values</text><text class="lab wn" x="20" y="154">exact, but the count explodes with maturity</text><line class="grid" x1="300" y1="14" x2="300" y2="176"/><text class="ttl" x="320" y="18">Monte Carlo &#8212; sample instead</text><line class="grid" x1="320" y1="26" x2="320" y2="122"/><line class="grid" x1="320" y1="122" x2="550" y2="122"/><path d="M326 106 L382 78 L438 92 L494 60" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width=".9" fill="none" opacity=".55"/><path d="M326 106 L382 96 L438 66 L494 48" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width=".9" fill="none" opacity=".55"/><path d="M326 106 L382 112 L438 88 L494 96" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width=".9" fill="none" opacity=".55"/><path d="M326 106 L382 88 L438 110 L494 84" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width=".9" fill="none" opacity=".55"/><path d="M326 106 L382 104 L438 76 L494 66" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width=".9" fill="none" opacity=".55"/><path d="M326 106 L382 120 L438 104 L494 118" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-width=".9" fill="none" opacity=".55"/><text class="lab" x="320" y="140">simulate many random paths, average the results</text><text class="lab ok" x="320" y="154">the only workable route for path-dependent cash flows</text><text class="lab bad" x="20" y="184">Monte Carlo is model-dependent: it will not reprice benchmarks unless a constant is added to force calibration</text>','Both average path values, and both need the same caveat. Monte Carlo does not automatically reprice observed bonds &mdash; a drift adjustment is added to force it, and without that the model is not arbitrage-free. Its advantage is path dependency, which is why mortgage-backed securities require it.',ARRAY['FI.LM2.f','FI.LM2.g']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM3.01','fi','The decomposition principle','Two equations shown as bars: a straight bond minus a call equals a callable bond; a straight bond plus a put equals a putable bond.','0 0 600 180','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="20">Every embedded option is owned by someone</text><rect class="bar-t" x="20" y="34" width="200" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="120" y="51" text-anchor="middle">Straight bond</text><text class="nt" x="232" y="51">&#8722;</text><rect class="bar-e" x="250" y="34" width="106" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="303" y="51" text-anchor="middle">Call option</text><text class="nt" x="368" y="51">=</text><rect class="node-c" x="386" y="34" width="150" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="461" y="51" text-anchor="middle">Callable bond</text><text class="lab bad" x="250" y="74">issuer owns it &#8212; so it is subtracted</text><rect class="bar-t" x="20" y="94" width="200" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="120" y="111" text-anchor="middle">Straight bond</text><text class="nt" x="232" y="111">+</text><rect class="bar-r" x="250" y="94" width="106" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="303" y="111" text-anchor="middle">Put option</text><text class="nt" x="368" y="111">=</text><rect class="node-c" x="386" y="94" width="150" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="461" y="111" text-anchor="middle">Putable bond</text><text class="lab ok" x="250" y="134">investor owns it &#8212; so it is added</text><text class="lab wn" x="20" y="164">get the sign from who holds the option, and every LM3 relationship follows from these two lines</text>','This is the most load-bearing fact in the module. The issuer holds the call, so its value is subtracted and a callable bond is always worth less than the equivalent straight bond. The investor holds the put, so it is added. Every duration, volatility and OAS result in LM3 is a consequence of these two signs.',ARRAY['FI.LM3.a']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM3.02','fi','Negative convexity','Two price-yield curves: a straight bond curving upward as yields fall, and a callable bond flattening below the strike yield as its price compresses toward the call price.','0 0 560 185','<line class="grid" x1="34" y1="26" x2="34" y2="128"/><line class="grid" x1="34" y1="128" x2="234" y2="128"/><text class="lab" x="134" y="143" text-anchor="middle">yield %</text><text class="lab" x="18" y="77" transform="rotate(-90 18 77)" text-anchor="middle">price</text><path d="M44.0 49.8 L46.7 52.9 L49.4 55.9 L52.1 58.8 L54.9 61.5 L57.6 64.2 L60.3 66.7 L63.0 69.2 L65.7 71.5 L68.4 73.8 L71.1 75.9 L73.9 78.0 L76.6 80.0 L79.3 81.9 L82.0 83.8 L84.7 85.5 L87.4 87.2 L90.1 88.9 L92.9 90.4 L95.6 91.9 L98.3 93.4 L101.0 94.7 L103.7 96.1 L106.4 97.3 L109.1 98.6 L111.9 99.7 L114.6 100.9 L117.3 101.9 L120.0 103.0 L122.7 104.0 L125.4 104.9 L128.1 105.9 L130.9 106.7 L133.6 107.6 L136.3 108.4 L139.0 109.2 L141.7 109.9 L144.4 110.7 L147.1 111.4 L149.9 112.0 L152.6 112.7 L155.3 113.3 L158.0 113.9 L160.7 114.4 L163.4 115.0 L166.1 115.5 L168.9 116.0 L171.6 116.5 L174.3 116.9 L177.0 117.4 L179.7 117.8 L182.4 118.2 L185.1 118.6 L187.9 119.0 L190.6 119.3 L193.3 119.7 L196.0 120.0 L198.7 120.3 L201.4 120.6 L204.1 120.9 L206.9 121.2 L209.6 121.5 L212.3 121.7 L215.0 122.0 L217.7 122.2 L220.4 122.5 L223.1 122.7 L225.9 122.9 L228.6 123.1 L231.3 123.3 L234.0 123.5" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-width="1.4" fill="none" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><path d="M44.0 59.5 L46.7 59.1 L49.4 58.8 L52.1 58.8 L54.9 61.5 L57.6 64.2 L60.3 66.7 L63.0 69.2 L65.7 71.5 L68.4 73.8 L71.1 75.9 L73.9 78.0 L76.6 80.0 L79.3 81.9 L82.0 83.8 L84.7 85.5 L87.4 87.2 L90.1 88.9 L92.9 90.4 L95.6 91.9 L98.3 93.4 L101.0 94.7 L103.7 96.1 L106.4 97.3 L109.1 98.6 L111.9 99.7 L114.6 100.9 L117.3 101.9 L120.0 103.0 L122.7 104.0 L125.4 104.9 L128.1 105.9 L130.9 106.7 L133.6 107.6 L136.3 108.4 L139.0 109.2 L141.7 109.9 L144.4 110.7 L147.1 111.4 L149.9 112.0 L152.6 112.7 L155.3 113.3 L158.0 113.9 L160.7 114.4 L163.4 115.0 L166.1 115.5 L168.9 116.0 L171.6 116.5 L174.3 116.9 L177.0 117.4 L179.7 117.8 L182.4 118.2 L185.1 118.6 L187.9 119.0 L190.6 119.3 L193.3 119.7 L196.0 120.0 L198.7 120.3 L201.4 120.6 L204.1 120.9 L206.9 121.2 L209.6 121.5 L212.3 121.7 L215.0 122.0 L217.7 122.2 L220.4 122.5 L223.1 122.7 L225.9 122.9 L228.6 123.1 L231.3 123.3 L234.0 123.5" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/><line x1="102.0" y1="26" x2="102.0" y2="128" stroke="var(--amber)" stroke-dasharray="2 3"/><text class="lab wn" x="102.0" y="22" text-anchor="middle">call becomes live</text><text class="lab" x="246" y="44" fill="var(--faint)">straight bond &#8212; positive convexity</text><text class="lab" x="246" y="62" fill="var(--cyan)">callable bond</text><text class="lab bad" x="246" y="88">below the strike yield the price</text><text class="lab bad" x="246" y="102">compresses toward the call price</text><text class="lab" x="246" y="124">upside is capped, downside is not</text><text class="lab wn" x="34" y="164">a putable bond does the opposite: its price floors at the put price, so convexity stays positive throughout</text>','As yields fall the issuer''s call moves into the money and the price stops rising &mdash; the curve bends the wrong way. That flattening is negative convexity, and it is why a callable bond''s effective duration falls as rates drop while a straight bond''s rises.',ARRAY['FI.LM3.f']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM3.03','fi','Volatility and the yield curve','A flow showing higher volatility raising both option values, which lowers a callable bond and raises a putable bond, followed by notes on how curve shape affects exercise likelihood.','0 0 620 190','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="20">Higher interest rate volatility</text><path class="edge edge-y" d="M22 32 V50" marker-end="url(#g)"/><text class="nt" x="40" y="48">option value rises &#8212; both calls and puts</text><path class="edge" d="M40 56 V74" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect class="node-r" x="20" y="74" width="270" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt bad" x="34" y="95">Callable bond value FALLS (straight &#8722; bigger call)</text><path class="edge" d="M300 91 H320" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect class="node-ok" x="320" y="74" width="280" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="334" y="95">Putable bond value RISES (straight + bigger put)</text><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="124" x2="600" y2="124"/><text class="ttl" x="20" y="144">Curve shape</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="162">upward-sloping: forwards are high, so the call is less likely to be exercised &#8212; call value low</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="178">flattening or inverting: forwards fall, exercise becomes likelier &#8212; call value rises, callable value falls</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','Volatility raises the value of any option, so the sign on the option in the decomposition determines the effect on the bond. Curve shape works through exercise probability instead: a steep upward curve means high forward rates and an unlikely call, while flattening pulls forwards down and makes exercise more probable.',ARRAY['FI.LM3.b','FI.LM3.c']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM3.04','fi','Z-spread versus OAS','A Z-spread bar splitting into an option-adjusted spread portion and an option cost portion, with the formulas for callable and putable bonds.','0 0 600 180','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="20">Both are constant additions to the spot curve &#8212; one includes the option</text><rect class="bar-t" x="20" y="34" width="380" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="210" y="51" text-anchor="middle">Z-spread</text><path class="edge" d="M120 60 V80" marker-end="url(#a)"/><path class="edge" d="M330 60 V80" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect class="node-ok" x="20" y="80" width="240" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="140" y="97" text-anchor="middle">OAS &#8212; credit and liquidity</text><rect class="node-r" x="266" y="80" width="134" height="26" rx="2"/><text class="nt bad" x="333" y="97" text-anchor="middle">option cost</text><text class="nt" x="420" y="52">Callable:</text><text class="lab" x="420" y="68">OAS = Z-spread &#8722; option cost</text><text class="nt" x="420" y="92">Putable:</text><text class="lab" x="420" y="108">OAS = Z-spread + option cost</text><text class="lab wn" x="20" y="134">OAS strips the option out, so it is the only spread that compares a callable bond with a straight one</text><text class="lab bad" x="20" y="152">OAS is volatility-dependent: assume higher volatility and the computed OAS falls</text><text class="lab ok" x="20" y="170">wider OAS than comparable bonds &#8594; undervalued; narrower &#8594; overvalued</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','OAS removes the option so that bonds with and without embedded options can be compared on credit and liquidity alone. The trap is that OAS is an output of an assumed volatility: raise the volatility input and the option cost rises, which mechanically shrinks the OAS you compute from the same market price.',ARRAY['FI.LM3.e']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM3.05','fi','Effective and key rate durations','Left: effective duration paths for a straight and a callable bond as yields fall. Right: a key rate duration profile with exposure concentrated near the call date, plus notes on one-sided durations.','0 0 640 225','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="18">Effective duration as rates fall</text><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="26" x2="20" y2="118"/><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="118" x2="210" y2="118"/><text class="lab" x="4" y="72" transform="rotate(-90 4 72)" text-anchor="middle">eff. dur.</text><path d="M26 40 L70 46 L114 58 L158 76 L202 92" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"/><path d="M26 96 L70 90 L114 74 L158 62 L202 56" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.8" fill="none"/><text class="lab" x="20" y="150" fill="var(--faint)">straight bond: duration rises as yields fall</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="164" fill="var(--cyan)">callable: duration falls &#8212; call approaches</text><text class="lab wn" x="20" y="182">effective duration of a callable &#8804; straight;</text><text class="lab wn" x="20" y="196">of a putable &#8804; straight too</text><line class="grid" x1="250" y1="14" x2="250" y2="212"/><text class="ttl" x="272" y="18">Key rate durations</text><line class="grid" x1="272" y1="26" x2="272" y2="118"/><line class="grid" x1="272" y1="118" x2="452" y2="118"/><rect class="barc" x="280" y="108" width="20" height="10"/><rect class="barc" x="310" y="96" width="20" height="22"/><rect class="barc" x="340" y="60" width="20" height="58"/><rect class="barc" x="370" y="44" width="20" height="74"/><rect class="barc" x="400" y="88" width="20" height="30"/><rect class="barc" x="430" y="106" width="20" height="12"/><text class="lab" x="290" y="132" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text class="lab" x="320" y="132" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text class="lab" x="350" y="132" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text class="lab" x="380" y="132" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text class="lab" x="410" y="132" text-anchor="middle">7</text><text class="lab" x="440" y="132" text-anchor="middle">10</text><text class="lab" x="272" y="152">maturity point &#183; exposure concentrated at the call date, not spread evenly</text><text class="lab ok" x="272" y="182">this is what effective duration cannot show you</text><text class="lab" x="480" y="46">One-sided durations</text><text class="lab" x="480" y="64">callable: down-duration &#60; up-duration</text><text class="lab" x="480" y="80">&#8212; price rises less than it falls</text><text class="lab" x="480" y="102">putable: up-duration &#60; down-duration</text><text class="lab wn" x="480" y="126">the asymmetry is the option</text><text class="lab wn" x="480" y="140">showing up in the numbers</text>','Effective duration assumes a parallel shift, so it averages away exactly the information an embedded option creates. Key rate durations show where along the curve the exposure actually sits, and one-sided durations expose the asymmetry: a callable bond rises less on a rate fall than it drops on an equivalent rise.',ARRAY['FI.LM3.g','FI.LM3.h']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM4.01','fi','The three inputs to credit risk','Three input boxes for probability of default, loss given default and exposure at default multiplying to expected loss, followed by the credit valuation adjustment definition.','0 0 600 195','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="20">Expected loss is a product, not a sum</text><rect class="node-c" x="20" y="34" width="120" height="46" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="80" y="54" text-anchor="middle">POD</text><text class="nm" x="80" y="70" text-anchor="middle">probability of default</text><rect class="node-c" x="158" y="34" width="120" height="46" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="218" y="54" text-anchor="middle">LGD</text><text class="nm" x="218" y="70" text-anchor="middle">loss given default</text><rect class="node-c" x="296" y="34" width="120" height="46" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="356" y="54" text-anchor="middle">EAD</text><text class="nm" x="356" y="70" text-anchor="middle">exposure at default</text><text class="nt" x="152" y="62">&#215;</text><text class="nt" x="290" y="62">&#215;</text><text class="nt" x="428" y="62">=</text><rect class="node-ok" x="444" y="34" width="140" height="46" rx="2"/><text class="nt ok" x="514" y="54" text-anchor="middle">Expected loss</text><text class="nm" x="514" y="70" text-anchor="middle">before discounting</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="104">LGD = 1 &#8722; recovery rate. A 40% recovery means an LGD of 60%.</text><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="118" x2="580" y2="118"/><text class="ttl" x="20" y="138">Credit valuation adjustment</text><text class="nt" x="20" y="158">CVA = &#931; PV(expected loss in each period)</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="176">value of a risky bond = value of the same bond risk-free &#8722; CVA</text><text class="lab wn" x="330" y="158">CVA rises as the credit curve steepens,</text><text class="lab wn" x="330" y="176">because later periods carry more default risk</text>','The three inputs multiply, so a change in any one scales the whole result. CVA is then the present value of those expected losses across every period, and a risky bond is worth the risk-free bond less that adjustment &mdash; which is why CVA and credit spread move together.',ARRAY['FI.LM4.a']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM4.02','fi','Structural and reduced-form models','Left: a firm''s asset value path crossing below the face value of debt, triggering default. Right: a reduced-form flow estimating default intensity from observable inputs.','0 0 640 215','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="18">Structural &#8212; default is an option outcome</text><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="26" x2="20" y2="136"/><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="136" x2="250" y2="136"/><text class="lab" x="135" y="151" text-anchor="middle">time</text><line x1="26" y1="98" x2="244" y2="98" stroke="var(--red)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text class="lab bad" x="26" y="112">debt face value</text><path d="M26 76 L62 58 L98 70 L134 44 L170 66 L206 92 L244 108" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/><text class="lab" x="26" y="44" fill="var(--cyan)">asset value</text><circle cx="236" cy="104" r="4" fill="var(--red)"/><text class="lab bad" x="232" y="130" text-anchor="end">assets fall below debt &#8594; default</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="156">equity = a call option on the firm&#8217;s assets</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="170">debt = risk-free debt &#8722; a put on the assets</text><text class="lab bad" x="20" y="188">needs the asset value, which is not observable</text><text class="lab bad" x="20" y="202">for a firm without traded equity</text><line class="grid" x1="300" y1="14" x2="300" y2="206"/><text class="ttl" x="320" y="18">Reduced-form &#8212; default is a statistical event</text><text class="lab" x="320" y="42">no view on why a firm defaults</text><text class="lab" x="320" y="58">a hazard rate is estimated from observable</text><text class="lab" x="320" y="74">variables and historical data</text><rect class="node" x="320" y="88" width="140" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="390" y="103" text-anchor="middle">Inputs</text><text class="nm" x="390" y="115" text-anchor="middle">macro + firm data</text><path class="edge" d="M460 105 H492" marker-end="url(#a)"/><rect class="node-c" x="492" y="88" width="124" height="34" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="554" y="103" text-anchor="middle">Default intensity</text><text class="nm" x="554" y="115" text-anchor="middle">fitted, not derived</text><text class="lab ok" x="320" y="146">uses observable inputs only</text><text class="lab ok" x="320" y="162">allows default probability to vary with the cycle</text><text class="lab bad" x="320" y="184">assumes the past predicts the future, and gives</text><text class="lab bad" x="320" y="200">no economic explanation of default</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','Structural models explain default &mdash; it happens when asset value falls below debt, making equity a call on assets. Reduced-form models decline to explain it and estimate the intensity statistically instead. The trade is explanatory power against observability: structural needs asset values you often cannot see.',ARRAY['FI.LM4.d']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM4.03','fi','The term structure of credit spreads','Three credit spread curves by quality: high yield sloping downward, investment grade sloping upward, and highest quality nearly flat.','0 0 560 180','<line class="grid" x1="34" y1="26" x2="34" y2="126"/><line class="grid" x1="34" y1="126" x2="244" y2="126"/><text class="lab" x="139" y="141" text-anchor="middle">maturity</text><text class="lab" x="18" y="76" transform="rotate(-90 18 76)" text-anchor="middle">spread (bps)</text><path d="M40 46 L96 40 L152 44 L208 52 L240 62" stroke="var(--red)" stroke-width="1.7" fill="none"/><path d="M40 104 L96 92 L152 82 L208 74 L240 70" stroke="var(--cyan)" stroke-width="1.7" fill="none"/><path d="M40 116 L96 110 L152 106 L208 103 L240 102" stroke="var(--green)" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"/><text class="lab bad" x="256" y="52">high yield &#8212; downward or humped</text><text class="lab" x="256" y="70" fill="var(--cyan)">investment grade &#8212; upward</text><text class="lab ok" x="256" y="88">highest quality &#8212; flat</text><text class="lab" x="256" y="112">a distressed issuer faces its</text><text class="lab" x="256" y="126">largest risk immediately, so</text><text class="lab" x="256" y="140">near-dated spreads are widest</text><text class="lab wn" x="34" y="164">spread curves also steepen in expansions and flatten or invert as credit conditions deteriorate</text>','Quality determines the shape. An investment grade issuer''s risk accumulates with time, so spreads widen with maturity. A distressed issuer''s greatest danger is surviving the next year at all, so its near-dated spreads are the widest and the curve slopes down &mdash; the opposite of intuition built on rate curves.',ARRAY['FI.LM4.f']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM5.01','fi','CDS mechanics','A protection buyer paying quarterly premiums to a protection seller, who pays out only if a credit event occurs.','0 0 620 195','<rect class="node-c" x="20" y="40" width="140" height="46" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="90" y="60" text-anchor="middle">Protection buyer</text><text class="nm" x="90" y="76" text-anchor="middle">short credit risk</text><rect class="node-c" x="400" y="40" width="140" height="46" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="470" y="60" text-anchor="middle">Protection seller</text><text class="nm" x="470" y="76" text-anchor="middle">long credit risk</text><path class="edge edge-n" d="M160 54 H396" marker-end="url(#m)"/><text class="el" x="278" y="48" text-anchor="middle">quarterly premium until default or maturity</text><path class="edge edge-y" d="M396 78 H164" marker-end="url(#g)"/><text class="el" x="278" y="94" text-anchor="middle">payout only if a credit event occurs</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="122">Credit events: bankruptcy, failure to pay, restructuring</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="138">Settlement: cash (notional &#215; LGD) or physical delivery of the reference obligation</text><text class="lab wn" x="20" y="160">standard coupons are fixed at 1% for investment grade and 5% for high yield</text><text class="lab wn" x="20" y="176">&#8212; the difference between that coupon and the fair spread is settled as an upfront payment</text><defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--faint)"/></marker><marker id="g" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--green)"/></marker><marker id="m" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--amber)"/></marker><marker id="c" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L8 4 L0 8 z" fill="var(--cyan)"/></marker></defs>','The buyer is short credit risk and pays a stream; the seller is long credit risk and pays only on a credit event. Because coupons are standardised at 1% or 5%, the contract almost never trades at fair value at inception, and the gap is settled upfront.',ARRAY['FI.LM5.a']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM5.02','fi','Upfront premium and mark to market','The upfront premium formula, and a chart of mark-to-market value rising as the credit spread widens.','0 0 620 200','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="18">At inception</text><text class="nt" x="20" y="42">Upfront &#8776; (fair spread &#8722; standard coupon) &#215; EffSpreadDur &#215; notional</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="60">spread above the coupon &#8594; buyer pays upfront &#183; spread below &#8594; seller pays</text><text class="lab" x="20" y="76">quoted as a price: upfront % = 100 &#8722; points upfront</text><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="90" x2="600" y2="90"/><text class="ttl" x="20" y="110">During the contract&#8217;s life</text><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="120" x2="20" y2="180"/><line class="grid" x1="20" y1="180" x2="250" y2="180"/><line x1="26" y1="152" x2="244" y2="152" stroke="var(--faint)" stroke-dasharray="2 3"/><path d="M26 152 L68 140 L110 146 L152 124 L194 132 L244 116" stroke="var(--green)" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/><text class="lab ok" x="26" y="196">spread widens &#8594; buyer gains</text><text class="nt" x="290" y="128">&#916;Value &#8776; &#916;spread &#215; EffSpreadDur &#215; notional</text><text class="lab" x="290" y="148">the buyer profits when credit deteriorates,</text><text class="lab" x="290" y="164">because protection bought cheaply is now dear</text><text class="lab wn" x="290" y="188">the seller&#8217;s position is the mirror image</text>','Both formulas are the same shape: a spread difference multiplied by effective spread duration and notional. At inception the difference is fair spread against the standard coupon; afterwards it is the change in spread since you traded. Widening spreads reward the protection buyer.',ARRAY['FI.LM5.b','FI.LM5.c']::text[],'generated'),
('FI.LM5.03','fi','CDS applications','Three boxes describing a basis trade, a curve trade and synthetic credit exposure using credit default swaps.','0 0 620 200','<text class="ttl" x="20" y="18">Three trades the exam asks about</text><rect class="node-c" x="20" y="30" width="580" height="46" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="34" y="50">Basis trade</text><text class="lab" x="34" y="68">CDS spread and bond spread should match. If the bond spread is wider, buy the bond and buy protection &#8212; a negative basis trade.</text><rect class="node-c" x="20" y="84" width="580" height="46" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="34" y="104">Curve trade</text><text class="lab" x="34" y="122">Long short-dated protection and short long-dated, or the reverse. A view on the shape of the credit curve rather than its level.</text><rect class="node-c" x="20" y="138" width="580" height="46" rx="2"/><text class="nt" x="34" y="158">Synthetic exposure</text><text class="lab" x="34" y="176">Selling protection replicates owning the bond without funding it. Buying protection on a bond you hold neutralises the credit risk.</text><text class="lab wn" x="20" y="194">a naked CDS position is a pure credit view; paired with the bond it becomes a relative-value or hedging trade</text>','All three exploit the same fact: a CDS and the underlying bond are two routes to the same credit exposure. When they disagree there is a basis to arbitrage; when the disagreement is across maturities there is a curve trade; and when you want the exposure without the funding, selling protection substitutes for owning the bond.',ARRAY['FI.LM5.d']::text[],'generated')
